      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working on a 12 week hypertrophy block for a client but they've hit a massive plateau with their back squat. they haven't added weight in 3 weeks and r feeling pretty sluggish. can u explain how implementing a deload or messing with volume could help them break through this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A deload week can be helpful for accumulated fatigue and muscle growth, allowing you to maintain the hypertrophy block but reduce workout stress to allow for better recovery and progress. With a volume-decrease deload, you could reduce the number of sets by one (e.g., from 4 sets to 3 sets), keep the weight lighter, but still maintain rep quality. The adjusted volume should still be enough to elicit enough muscular tension to promote hypertrophy, while still offering reduced time under tension that might help to bypass the plateau. \n\nAlternatively, a full-body strategy that incorporates the back squat once a week instead of twice, or once every other week, can emphasis other key lifts to stimulate hormonal and neural advantages across other body parts. If you're dedicated to a back squat specialist approach, then once a week may involve an unweighted, lower volume back squat, a row, chest press, and deadlift, aiming to maintain intensity with lighter load or reduced volume to stimulate hypertrophy. The idea is to make sufficient progress and movement variety on specific back squat days to encourage continued performance. Meanwhile, be sure to track precise test-retest numbers to monitor progress and how morale is impacted by sub max lifts. If your client struggles with the del",
      "source": "target",
