    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand the perplexity some clients feel during a strength plateau - they can truly hear it in their saying that their \"drive\" has been sapped or their strength is being shackled by some unseen force.\n\nFrom a physiological standpoint, a strength plateau can be illustrative of reciprocal fatigue, where muscles ultimately responsible for the specific lift become exhausted and cannot maintain tension or strength consistently. It can also highlight a number of dysfunctional movement patterns perhaps not evident at higher volume or intensity, in turn, hindering maximal force production, disrupting efficient hypertrophy, or even affecting spinal alignment or form due to built-up fatigue in the body's systems.\n\nNervous system fatigue, as mentioned earlier, hampers motor unit recruitment thresholds, primarily with the higher-threshold motor units, responsible for generating most of the certified power in movements like a back squat, because the body becomes incapable of transmitting adequate signaling.\n\nEntering the power or \"motor learning\" phase of strength training, with a week or two focused on slightly heavier weights but compared to lower volume, often helps rejuvenate your client's CNS from the built-up burnout and drive feedback. It's also worth noting that practitioners' varied energy levels, sleep quality, and hormonal responses may significantly impact performance, as well. Periodized programs account",
